{
  "dom_id": "step-q183471",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Negate the following statement: \n\n$\\,$ At least one prime number is even.",
  "worked_solution": "Let $P$ be the set of all prime numbers. The given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists $p\\in P$ such that $p$ is even.\n\nIntroducing the existential quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\,$ $∃p\\in P,\\,p\\text{ is even}.$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∃p\\in P,\\,p\\text{ is even}) & ≡∀p\\in P,\\,¬(p\\text{ is even}) \\\\  & ≡∀p\\in P,\\,p\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich reads \n\n$\\,$ For all $p\\in P,$ $p$ is odd.\n\nor simply\n\n$\\,$ Every prime number is odd.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
